; Scry core grammar -- Phase 1 subset.
;
; Covers a deliberately bounded slice of lang_spec.md so the core/variant
; composition architecture (impl_spec.md §2/§4) can be proven against a
; real parser before the full language is written out. Phase 1 has:
;
;   - `#` line comments (lang_spec §3) -- `;` block comments deferred
;   - bare identifiers + dot-path (§3) -- backtick-escaping deferred
;   - double-quoted strings, integers, decimals, `2/3`-shaped rational
;     literals, radix integers (`0x`/`0o`/`0b`), nil/true/false (§4) --
;     everything else in §4 (multiline strings, regex sigils, atoms,
;     lists as a literal kind, duration/byte-size/date literals, and the
;     rest of the exact-rational tower -- arithmetic *expressions*,
;     inexact/float coexistence -- see ScryCore.Rational's own
;     moduledoc) deferred
;   - `select`/`where`, `and`/`or`/`not`, comparisons, `in [...]`, and the
;     full §5.2 header-modifier chain (`group by`/`having`/`distinct`/
;     `order by`/`limit ... offset`, in their required fixed order) (§5)
;     -- `group by ... rollup`/`... cube`, the "having requires an
;     aggregate expression in the body" compile-time check (nothing
;     checks it yet -- there's no such thing as an aggregate expression
;     in the grammar yet either), and the rest of §5's keyword reference
;     deferred; all of §6/§7/§8/§9 deferred
;   - one EP1(a) extension point (`select_ep1a`, lang_spec §2), proving
;     the union-of-simultaneous-kind-contributions composition pattern
;     the whole design actually needs (not "core references a rule it
;     never defines" -- core supplies a real always-fails default, see
;     `select_ep1a`'s own comment below for why that turned out to
;     matter)
;
; Every deferral above is a real gap, not a design decision -- each one
; needs its own pass. See impl_spec.md §8 for how this file's own
; composition gets tested (ScryCore.GrammarCompose + its test suite).

@grammar "scry_core"
@root select
@case_insensitive
@skip TRIVIA

; ---- trivia ----

COMMENT := "#" (!"\n" .)*
TRIVIA  := (SPACE | COMMENT)*

; ---- identifiers / keywords ----
;
; IDENT feeds keyword reclassification below -- every structural keyword
; this phase needs is a *reclassified* IDENT match, not its own token,
; so declaration order between them and IDENT never matters (only the
; reclassification does). No backtick-escape mechanism yet (deferred,
; see file header): a query that needs "select" etc. as a plain field
; name has no way to write it in this phase.
;
; @refine, not @keywords: @keywords' table lookup is an exact,
; case-sensitive match against the token's raw text, and does not pick
; up @case_insensitive -- confirmed empirically, not a guess (see
; ScryCore.Grammar.KeywordRefiner's moduledoc). @refine hands the whole
; decision to that module instead, which downcases first.

IDENT := [[:alpha:]_][[:alnum:]_-]*
  @refine("ScryCore.Grammar.KeywordRefiner", "refine",
          KW_SELECT, KW_WHERE, KW_AND, KW_OR, KW_NOT, KW_IN,
          KW_NIL, KW_TRUE, KW_FALSE,
          KW_GROUP, KW_BY, KW_HAVING, KW_DISTINCT, KW_ORDER,
          KW_LIMIT, KW_OFFSET, KW_DESC, KW_ASC)

; ---- literals ----
;
; Three mutually-exclusive number tokens, disambiguated by maximal munch
; alone (Aether's own lexer rule: longest match at a given position wins
; -- see guides/aether/AETHER.md's "Maximal munch") -- declaration order
; between them is irrelevant, since a given input position can only ever
; actually match one of the three shapes to begin with:
;
;   - DECIMAL requires a literal "." (`DIGIT+ "." DIGIT+`), so it simply
;     doesn't match plain digits at all (`30` never reaches DECIMAL,
;     only INTEGER)
;   - RADIX requires the literal "0" + a base letter, strictly longer
;     than the single "0" digit INTEGER alone could match at that same
;     position (`0x1F`: RADIX consumes all 4 characters, INTEGER only 1)
;
; `[xX]`/`[oO]`/`[bB]` spell out both cases explicitly rather than
; relying on `@case_insensitive` -- that pragma only affects bare
; *string* literals (`"lit"`), never character classes, per the same
; guide's own `@case_insensitive` section.
INTEGER := DIGIT+
DECIMAL := DIGIT+ "." DIGIT+
RADIX   := "0" [xX] HEX+
         | "0" [oO] [0-7]+
         | "0" [bB] [01]+
STRING  := "\"" (!"\"" .)* "\""

; ---- operators / delimiters ----

SLASH  := "/"
NOT_EQ := "not="
LE     := "<="
GE     := ">="
EQ     := "="
LT     := "<"
GT     := ">"
LBRACE := "{"
RBRACE := "}"
LBRACK := "["
RBRACK := "]"
COMMA  := ","
DOT    := "."

; `[]` (an empty character class) matches no character, ever -- the
; "always fails" default an extension point needs when nothing has
; contributed to it (see `select_ep1a` below). Deliberately not a
; `!`-negated lookahead on some "any character" token: that combination
; means "always *succeeds*, with zero width" (there being no character
; left to fail to find only happens at true end of input) -- the
; opposite of what's wanted here, confirmed empirically before settling
; on this instead.
NEVER := []

; ---- grammar ----

; Modifiers appear in lang_spec.md §5.2/§6's own fixed order, mirroring
; evaluation order front to back: an EP1(a) modifier (narrows/reshapes
; the source) runs first, then where (filters), group by (groups),
; having (filters groups), distinct (dedupes the projection), order by
; (sorts), limit ... offset (paginates last).
select := KW_SELECT source:path select_ep1a? where_clause? group_by_clause?
          having_clause? distinct_clause? order_by_clause? limit_clause?
          LBRACE body:body_list RBRACE

; core's EP1(a) header-modifier extension point (lang_spec §2, §5.2).
; Unlike the language spec's own text, this is *not* "exactly one kind
; may ever fill this" -- §5.2 itself has `last` (time-series) and
; `deep` (document) both nominating the same position, and a build can
; legitimately load both kinds at once. `select_ep1a` therefore always
; has a real definition (`NEVER` immediately below, when no kind
; contributes here) that `ScryCore.GrammarCompose.merge/2` unions each
; loaded fragment's own contribution into, rather than a dangling
; reference exactly one fragment is allowed to supply. See that
; module's own moduledoc for the full reasoning and
; `ScryCore.GrammarComposeTest` for both a one-fragment and a
; two-simultaneous-fragment proof.
select_ep1a := NEVER

; Every optional (`?`-suffixed) *rule* reference below is deliberately
; left unrenamed (`select_ep1a?`, `where_clause?`, `literal_list?` --
; never e.g. `where:where_clause?`), for the same empirically-found
; reason: explicitly naming an Opt-wrapped bare rule reference produces
; a *second*, separate raw-text capture under the explicit name
; alongside the properly dispatched one under the rule's own implicit
; self-capture name -- not a single renamed capture the way the rest of
; Aether's naming rules would suggest. Worse, the two also differ in
; whether "absent" is even detectable the same way: a *renamed* optional
; always produces a capture (raw text `""` when absent); the *bare*
; form produces no capture key at all when absent, only ever appearing
; when it actually matched. ScryCore.Actions relies on exactly that
; distinction (`Map.has_key?/2`, not an `== ""` check) -- see its own
; moduledoc. Does not apply to optional *token* references (`KW_NOT?`
; below keeps its name fine) -- empirically, only rule references have
; this problem.

; select_ep1a := <supplied by a kind fragment>

where_clause := KW_WHERE cond:predicate

; `... rollup`/`... cube` (lang_spec §5.2) not yet supported -- this is
; always the "plain" group-by shape.
group_by_clause := KW_GROUP KW_BY fields:field_list

having_clause := KW_HAVING cond:predicate

; A bare keyword, no argument -- `select`'s own `distinct_clause?`
; reference only ever needs to detect *presence*, not read a value out
; of this rule, so no named capture is needed here (same
; single-capture-passthrough default `comp_op`'s own bare token
; alternatives already rely on).
distinct_clause := KW_DISTINCT

order_by_clause := KW_ORDER KW_BY items:order_item_list
order_item_list := head:order_item (COMMA tail:order_item)*

; `dir:(KW_DESC | KW_ASC)?` -- a *named, optional parenthesized choice*
; of tokens -- confirmed empirically (scratch grammar, not assumed) to
; behave like every other optional *token* reference in this file
; (`neg:KW_NOT?`): absence is a real capture, the literal empty string
; `""`, checked by value -- not the `Map.has_key?/2` check a bare
; optional *rule* reference needs. It also produces a second, harmless
; spurious capture under whichever of `KW_DESC`/`KW_ASC` actually
; matched (same class of finding as `select_ep1a?`'s own history, just
; not a problem here since ScryCore.Actions only ever reads `dir`).
order_item := field:path dir:(KW_DESC | KW_ASC)?

limit_clause  := KW_LIMIT n:INTEGER offset_clause?
offset_clause := KW_OFFSET n:INTEGER

field_list := head:path (COMMA tail:path)*

predicate   := disjunction
disjunction := left:conjunction (KW_OR right:conjunction)*
conjunction := left:negation (KW_AND right:negation)*
negation    := neg:KW_NOT? expr:comparison

comparison := left:path op:comp_op right:literal
            | left:path in:KW_IN LBRACK literal_list? RBRACK

comp_op := LE | GE | NOT_EQ | EQ | LT | GT

literal_list := head:literal (COMMA tail:literal)*

; `rational` before bare INTEGER: both start with the same INTEGER
; token, so ordered-PEG-choice would otherwise never get a chance to try
; the SLASH-containing alternative -- ordinary backtracking makes this
; safe (see priv/grammar.aether's own `body_item` comment for the same
; convention elsewhere). Deliberately plain-INTEGER-only on both sides
; (not DECIMAL) -- lang_spec.md §4's own example is `2/3`, not a
; division of two arbitrary exact rationals, which would need real
; division, not just `ScryCore.Rational.new/2`'s construct-and-reduce.
literal := STRING | rational | DECIMAL | RADIX | INTEGER | KW_NIL | KW_TRUE | KW_FALSE

rational := numerator:INTEGER SLASH denominator:INTEGER

path := head:IDENT (DOT tail:IDENT)*

; A body item is a nested SELECT (core-only, no extension needed --
; ordinary PEG recursion, same rule referencing itself indirectly
; through body_list), a kind-contributed construct (EP1(b)/(c)/(d)
; body-item shapes -- a block-opening construct, a scoped pseudo-field,
; or a bare value pseudo-field; lang_spec §2's impl_spec companion),
; or a plain field path -- tried in that order (most specific leading
; token first, generic fallback last; PEG's first-match-wins doesn't
; actually need the ordering here, since all three start with
; structurally distinct tokens, but it's the same defensive convention
; select_ep1a's own alternatives follow).
body_item := select | body_item_ep1 | path

; core's EP1(b)/(c)/(d) body-item extension point -- the same
; union-of-simultaneous-contributions design as select_ep1a (this
; file's own comment there has the full reasoning); a real kind
; contributes its own block-opening construct, pseudo-field, or value
; pseudo-field here (VIA, PARENT, SIBLINGS, ANCESTORS, PATH, ... in the
; language spec's own vocabulary), and more than one kind can
; contribute simultaneously.
body_item_ep1 := NEVER

body_list := head:body_item (COMMA tail:body_item)*
